{{Listen|filename=Stefani-2004-hollaback-girl.ogg|title="Hollaback Girl"|description=Stefani sings in a chant during the chorus for "Hollaback Girl"|format=[[Ogg]]}}
"Hollaback Girl" is a [[Pop music|pop]],<ref>{{Cite web |last=Magnocavallo |first=Fabio |date=October 20, 2022 |title=Musicians Who Slammed Famous People In Their Lyrics |url=https://www.nickiswift.com/1062789/musicians-who-slammed-famous-people-in-their-lyrics/ |access-date=May 27, 2026 |website=Nicki Swift |publisher=[[Static Media]]}}</ref> [[Hip hop music|hip-hop]]<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=Jamison |first=Darren |date=2023-03-30 |title=10 Best Gwen Stefani Songs of All Time |url=https://singersroom.com/best-gwen-stefani-songs-of-all-time/ |access-date=2025-06-25 |website=Singersroom |language=en-US}}</ref> and [[post-R&B]] song,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Empire |first=Kitty |date=2006-12-03 |title=How do you solve a problem like Stefani? |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2006/dec/03/popandrock.gwenstefani |access-date=2025-06-25 |work=[[The Guardian]] |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> that draws influence from [[1980s in music#Hip hop|1980s hip-hop]] and dance music.<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Caulfield |first=Keith |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=whQEAAAAMBAJ&q=gwen+stefani+hollaback+girl&pg=PA68 |title=Reviews: Spotlights |magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] |volume=117 |issue=28 |page=68 |date=July 9, 2005 |issn=0006-2510 |access-date=February 4, 2016 |via=[[Google Books]]}}</ref><ref name="Contact">{{cite magazine |url=http://www.contactmusic.com/pages/gwenstefanix28x04x05 |title=Gwen Stefani – Hollaback Girl – Video Streams |magazine=[[Contactmusic.com]] |date=April 28, 2005 |access-date=January 10, 2007 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151208002221/http://www.contactmusic.com/pages/gwenstefanix28x04x05 |archive-date=December 8, 2015}}</ref> A moderately fast song, with a [[tempo]] of 110 [[beats per minute]], it is played in the [[key (music)|key]] of [[D-sharp minor|D{{sharp}} minor]].<ref name="Digital Sheet Music, Gwen Stefani Hollaback Girl">{{cite web|url=http://www.musicnotes.com/sheetmusic/mtd.asp?ppn=MN0145251|title=Digital Sheet Music, Gwen Stefani 'Hollaback Girl'|url-access=subscription |publisher=Musicnotes.com|year=2005|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160916202602/http://www.musicnotes.com/sheetmusic/mtd.asp?ppn=MN0145251|archive-date=September 16, 2016}}</ref> Like the majority of [[pop music]], it is set in [[time signature|common time]]. The main [[chord progression|chord pattern]] of the song alternates between [[B major]] and [[D-sharp minor|D{{music|sharp}} minor]] [[triad (music)|triads]].<ref name="Digital Sheet Music, Gwen Stefani Hollaback Girl" /> Most of the harmonic content of the song revolves around a two-chord alternation which music theorists may regard as an L (leading tone) transformation, in which the root of the major chord is lowered by a half-step to form a second inversion minor chord on the third scale degree, a slight tonicization of B major, but resolving back to D{{sharp}} minor by having the same A{{sharp}}, [[dominant (music)|a perfect fourth down]] from D{{sharp}}. This stepwise motion between B and A{{music|sharp}} highlights this chord change. 

The song is in [[verse-chorus form]] with a [[bridge (music)|bridge]] before the fourth and last [[refrain|chorus]]. The song features sparse instrumentation, primarily a minimal [[beat (music)|beat]] produced by [[drum machine]].<ref>{{cite web|last=Slomowicz|first=DJ Ron|title=Superstars #1 Hits Remixed – Hosh Gureli Interview|work=About.com Entertainment |url=http://dancemusic.about.com/od/remixersproducers/a/SuperHoshGurInt_2.htm|url-status=live|archive-url=http://archive.wikiwix.com/cache/20110704175001/http://dancemusic.about.com/od/remixersproducers/a/SuperHoshGurInt_2.htm|archive-date=July 4, 2011|access-date=January 15, 2007|publisher=[[About.com]]}}</ref><ref name="Allmusic review">{{cite web |last=Erlewine |first=Stephen Thomas |author-link=Stephen Thomas Erlewine |url=http://www.allmusic.com/album/loveangelmusicbaby-mw0000258810 |title=Love.Angel.Music.Baby. – Gwen Stefani |website=[[AllMusic]] |access-date=February 3, 2016 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130214071051/http://www.allmusic.com/album/loveangelmusicbaby-mw0000258810 |archive-date=February 14, 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |last=Conway |first=Cat |url=http://drownedinsound.com/releases/6600/reviews/12352-gwen-stefani-hollaback-girl |title=Gwen Stefani – Hollaback Girl |magazine=[[Drowned in Sound]] |date=June 1, 2005 |access-date=January 15, 2007 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090826065921/http://drownedinsound.com/releases/6600/reviews/12352-gwen-stefani-hollaback-girl |archive-date=August 26, 2009}}</ref> A [[guitar]] plays the song's [[riff]], a six-note pattern as Stefani repeats "this my shit" during the chorus, and a [[brass section]] joins during the second chorus.<ref name="NationalReview" /> In part because of its cheerleading [[motif (music)|motif]], it drew comparisons to [[Toni Basil]]'s 1982 song "[[Mickey (Toni Basil song)|Mickey]]".<ref>{{cite web |last=Smith |first=RJ |url=http://www.blender.com/guide/new/53197/love-angel-music-baby.html |title=Gwen Stefani – Love. Angel. Music. Baby. |work=[[Blender (magazine)|Blender]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090522050828/http://www.blender.com/guide/new/53197/love-angel-music-baby.html |archive-date=May 22, 2009 |access-date=January 25, 2007}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |last=Huff |first=Quentin B. |url=http://www.popmatters.com/review/gwen-stefani-the-sweet-escape/ |title=Gwen Stefani: The Sweet Escape |magazine=[[PopMatters]] |date=December 14, 2006 |access-date=February 4, 2016 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160701000211/http://www.popmatters.com/review/gwen-stefani-the-sweet-escape/ |archive-date=July 1, 2016}}</ref>

